      "message": "Yes, exactly — that was the design intent. There is a follow-up series that does this for `aiReview` and finishes off the `ActionJson` cleanup:\n\n* [change 586901] — Switch aiReview to standard default-deny semantics + seed an explicit Registered Users grant in All-Projects (bootstrap + `Schema_187`). This drops the ActionJson smuggling from change 574541 along with the default-allow special case in RefControl.\n* [change 586902] — Extend `ChangePermissionsInfo` (the DTO returned by this endpoint) with an `ai_review` field.\n* [change 586903] — PolyGerrit: read aiReview from /permissions instead of `revisionActions.aiReview`.\n* [change 586904] — Move the parallel Follow-Up injection out of `ActionJson` too. Follow-Up carries change state (`!isAbandoned`) rather than a permission, so it goes to `gr-change-actions` directly — adding a `can_follow_up` field to /permissions would have type-confused the endpoint by mixing state with ACLs.\n\nAfter the series, `ActionJson` is back to being a pure `UiAction` → `ActionInfo` mapper with no client-side action special-casing.",
